---
import { SitePage } from '@boombapbot/editor';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Docs · boombapbot"
  description="How to use the boombapbot 808: writing rhythms, playing them by hand, rhythm tracks, the knobs and switches, and exporting."
>
  <SitePage client:load baseUrl={base} current="docs">
    <h1>How to use the 808</h1>
    <p class="sb-lede">
      Press <strong>START/STOP</strong> and it plays. Everything else on the faceplate changes what it plays or
      how it sounds.
    </p>

    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>
      The machine remembers sixteen <strong>rhythms</strong>, one for each step button: twelve Basic Rhythms and
      four Intro/Fill-ins. Each rhythm is one measure and has two versions, A and B. A rhythm is made of twelve
      <strong>tracks</strong>, one for the accent and one for each of eleven instruments, and each track is a row
      of steps that are on or off.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>mode selector</strong>, top left, decides what the sixteen step buttons do. In the two write
      modes they are the steps of the instrument you have selected. In the others they choose which rhythm
      plays.
    </p>

    <h2>Writing a rhythm</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Turn the mode selector to <strong>1st PART</strong>.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>START/STOP</strong>. The lamps run across the steps.</li>
      <li>Turn the instrument selector to an instrument, such as BD for the bass drum.</li>
      <li>Press the step buttons where it should play. A lit lamp is a step that is on; press it again to turn it off.</li>
      <li>Choose another instrument and carry on. The buttons always show the instrument the selector is on.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>AC</strong> on the selector is the accent. Every instrument that plays on an accented step is
      louder, by as much as the accent's level knob says.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>TAP</strong> writes too: while the rhythm runs, press it in time and the selected instrument is
      written on the step that is playing.
    </p>

    <h3>Choosing which rhythm to write</h3>
    <p>
      Turn the mode selector to <strong>PATTERN CLEAR</strong> and press a step button: 1 to 12 for a Basic
      Rhythm, the last four for an Intro/Fill-in. Its lamp lights. Pressing <strong>CLEAR</strong> there empties
      it. Then go back to 1st PART to write it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>BASIC VARIATION</strong> lever chooses version A or B of a Basic Rhythm, and the
      <strong>I/F VARIATION</strong> lever does the same for an Intro/Fill-in. With the lever on AB, CLEAR empties
      both versions.
    </p>

    <h3>Length and step size</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>A shorter measure</dt>
      <dd>
        In a write mode, press <strong>CLEAR</strong> so it glows, then press the step button that should be the
        last step. Press CLEAR again, or <kbd>Esc</kbd>, to let it up without changing anything.
      </dd>
      <dt>Steps to the beat</dt>
      <dd>
        Set the <strong>PRE-SCALE</strong> switch, then press CLEAR in a write mode to store it in the rhythm.
        Positions 1 to 4 give three, six, four and eight steps to the beat. Twelve steps at position 1 is a bar
        of triplets.
      </dd>
      <dt>More than sixteen steps</dt>
      <dd>
        Turn the mode selector to <strong>2nd PART</strong> and set its last step the same way. The 2nd part
        plays after the 1st, and the same sixteen buttons write it while the selector is there. Sixteen steps and
        four more is a bar of five beats.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>The sounds</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each column has a <strong>LEVEL</strong> knob. Some have TONE, DECAY, TUNING or SNAPPY as well.</li>
      <li>
        The five small switches swap an instrument for another: the three toms for congas, the rim shot for
        claves, and the hand clap for maracas. The steps stay where they are.
      </li>
      <li><strong>MASTER VOLUME</strong>, bottom right, is the whole machine.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>TEMPO</strong> turns in forty clicks from 40 to 300 beats a minute, and <strong>FINE</strong>
        moves it up to one click either way.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Drag a knob up or down to turn it, or focus it and use the arrow keys. The dials with fixed positions, such
      as the mode and instrument selectors, turn to wherever you press around them; you can also press their
      labels.
    </p>

    <h2>Playing by hand</h2>
    <p>Turn the mode selector to <strong>MANUAL PLAY</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The Basic Rhythm that will play flashes. Press another to choose it, then START/STOP.</li>
      <li>
        Choose another while it plays and it takes over at the end of the measure. If you choose it during the
        first beat, it takes over on the second beat of the same measure.
      </li>
      <li>The BASIC VARIATION lever plays version A, version B, or the two in turn on AB.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>TAP</strong> brings in the selected Intro/Fill-in once. Before you start, it is the intro. While
        it plays, it follows the measure that is running. Its button flashes while it waits; press TAP again to
        take it back.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>AUTO FILL IN</strong> dial plays the fill-in by itself every 2, 4, 8, 12 or 16 measures.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Rhythm tracks</h2>
    <p>
      A rhythm track is a recording of which rhythm played in each measure. There are twelve, and in the two
      track modes the instrument selector chooses one of them.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Turn the mode selector to <strong>COMPOSE</strong> and the instrument selector to a track.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>CLEAR</strong> to empty it.</li>
      <li>
        Press START/STOP and play as you would by hand, choosing rhythms and tapping in fill-ins. Every measure
        is remembered.
      </li>
      <li>Press START/STOP in the measure that should be the last.</li>
      <li>Turn the mode selector to <strong>PLAY</strong> and press START/STOP to hear it. It starts over at the end.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A track remembers rhythms, not versions, so the variation levers still work while it plays. A track holds
      64 measures and runs on into the next one when it is full. To change a track without starting again,
      compose over it without pressing CLEAR: it plays back, and the rhythm you choose replaces what was there
      from that measure on.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping what you make</h2>
    <p>
      Everything is saved in this browser as you go. The cog in the footer opens settings, where you can name
      your beat, undo and redo, put the starter rhythms back, and import or export. With a free account you can
      also save up to 20 beats to it from the account button in the footer, and load them on another device.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Project file</strong> holds everything the machine remembers, and opens here again.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>WAV and MIDI</strong> hold the rhythm the panel is on, as a loop: one measure, or A then B with
        its lever on AB. With the mode selector on PLAY or COMPOSE they hold the rhythm track instead.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What comes with it</h2>
    <p>
      A new machine has Basic Rhythms 1 to 8 written, Basic Rhythm 12 with every sound at once for setting
      levels, the four Intro/Fill-ins, and a rhythm track in track 1. Basic Rhythms 9 to 11 are empty.
    </p>

    <h2>Keyboard</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><kbd>Space</kbd></dt>
      <dd>Start or stop.</dd>
      <dt>Arrow keys</dt>
      <dd>Move between step buttons, and turn the knob or switch that has focus.</dd>
      <dt><kbd>Enter</kbd></dt>
      <dd>Press the button that has focus.</dd>
      <dt><kbd>Esc</kbd></dt>
      <dd>Let CLEAR up, or close a window.</dd>
      <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> or <kbd>Cmd</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd></dt>
      <dd>Undo. Add <kbd>Shift</kbd> to redo.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>The power switch</h2>
    <p>
      It turns the machine off: the lamps go dark, it stops, and nothing else on it answers until you switch it
      back on. Nothing it remembers is lost.
    </p>
  </SitePage>
</Base>
